From: Alsey Coleman Miller <alseycmiller@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PowerPC] Default to the secure PLT on 32-bit Linux

32-bit PowerPC code uses the secure PLT only where isPPC32SecurePlt()
says so: FreeBSD 13 and later, NetBSD, OpenBSD and musl. On glibc Linux
clang and the backend fall back to the old BSS PLT: calls are emitted as
"bl foo@plt" with no addend while r30 holds the -fPIC .got2 pointer.

lld implements only the secure PLT. For an addend of zero its call stub
reads the PLT slot relative to r30 as if r30 were the GOT, so every such
call from a -fPIC object jumps through the wrong slot; the Swift runtime
crashes in its first pthread_mutex_lock call. GNU toolchains on Linux
have defaulted to the secure PLT for years (GCC --enable-secureplt, as
Buildroot and the distributions configure it), so the BSS PLT also fails
to interoperate with them.

Treat every Linux libc the way musl is already treated.
---
--- a/llvm/include/llvm/TargetParser/Triple.h
+++ b/llvm/include/llvm/TargetParser/Triple.h
@@ -1077,7 +1077,7 @@
             ((getOS() == Triple::FreeBSD &&
               (getOSMajorVersion() >= 13 || getOSVersion().empty())) ||
              getOS() == Triple::NetBSD || getOS() == Triple::OpenBSD ||
-             isMusl()));
+             getOS() == Triple::Linux));
   }
 
   /// Tests whether the target is 32-bit RISC-V.
